    "author_association": "NONE",
    "body": "### Contributing guidelines\n\n- [X] I have read [CONTRIBUTING.md](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/main/CONTRIBUTING.md)\n- [X] I have read [CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/main/CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md)\n\n### Module(s)\n\ntelescope\n\n### Description\n\nFirst, congrats on mini, really **really** impressive project !\r\n\r\nI'm not a fan of having a tabline. So when using lunarvim, I disabled bufferline and went with <S-{h,l}> for buffer control + telescope for accessing already opened buffers and non-open files.\r\nI first thought I should make my own, less \"_bloated_\", configuration + custom plugins for statusline and buffer management. \r\nAs I started my project, I ran into yours. Mini.nvim directly spoke to me with its lightweight, handmade aspect and philosophy. \r\n\r\nI would love to contribute by writing this plugin, with following functionalities:\r\n- floating menu to select buffers:\r\n    - Either by using j,k,<Enter>\r\n    - Or with hop like access where each buffer is assigned a custom letter\r\n        > a |  first_file.lua\r\n        > d |  second_file.lua\r\n- floating menu with unopened file in current directory (same selection method as previous menu)\r\n    - possibility to set a depth in configuration\r\n    - possibility to blacklist certain files/directories\r\n- remapping for buffer navigation (imo leader based is better)\r\n    |keymap | leader based keymap | command                                          |\r\n    |-----------|-------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------|\r\n    | \\<S-l\\>  |  \\<leader\\>bh              |  :bn                                                    |\r\n    | \\<S-h\\> |  \\<leader\\>bl               |  :bp                                                    |\r\n    | \\<C-b\\> |  \\<leader\\>bb              | :MiniBuffers.open_select_buffer()     |\r\n    | \\<C-f\\>  |  \\<leader\\>bf               | :MiniBuffers.open_new_buffer()        |",
